---
// Project setup, step 5 — Obligations.
//
// THE STEP THIS IS. Steps 1–4 build the chain: source documents → commitments →
// requirements → actions. Step 5 is where the standing duties that fall out of that
// chain get scoped to this project. 402 of them, extracted from four real permit
// documents (ITP 177, EIR 129, ITP Amendment 36, USFWS BiOp 33).
//
// THE REGISTRY IS NOT A PAGE OF ITS OWN — it lives here, inside setup (team decision,
// 2026-09-03). The flat 402-row data catalog is the filing cabinet; this hierarchical
// view is the registry. A standalone full-registry page is expected LATER; when it
// lands it should reuse BcnRegistryTree without the confirm layer.
//
// WHY SUBJECT IS THE SPINE, and why not activity or commitment: see the header of
// src/data/obligation-registry.ts, which carries the measurements. The short version is
// that construction activities in Beacon are per-project and hand-authored
// (ProjectConstructionActivity), so there is no fixed list to tick and the project
// writes its own on an earlier setup page; and commitments are already step 2's job.
//
// THE TREE IS THE SHAPE, AND IT IS ONE REUSABLE COMPONENT. BcnRegistryTree takes a
// `mode`: `review` here, `browse` on the read-only full-registry page expected later.
// Same tree, same records; the read-only home simply passes the other mode rather than
// getting a stripped copy. A two-pane alternative is kept alongside at
// /prototypes/setup-obligations-panes.
//
// BUILT AS A STANDALONE PAGE, NOT INSIDE A WIZARD SHELL. There is no wizard shell in
// this repo — BcnSetupWizardCard only advertises four steps and every step href is a
// '#setup-*' fragment. Building the shell here would also force a fifth entity color
// into the setup ramp (source navy / commitment purple / requirement magenta / action
// pink), which is a hub token decision, not a prototype's to make. The step is named in
// the breadcrumb and the badge instead.
//
// WHAT IS REAL: every duty, heading, subject area, class, observable condition, gate
// flag and value-conflict flag is registry data. WHAT IS EXAMPLE: the project and
// tenant names in the shell, and the fact that nothing has been confirmed yet.
import AppShell from '../../layouts/AppShell.astro';
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import EsaBadge from '@esa/ecology/esa-badge.astro';
import BcnRegistryProgress from '../../components/bcn/BcnRegistryProgress.astro';
import BcnRegistryTree from '../../components/bcn/BcnRegistryTree.astro';

/** Not in esa-icon's registry; passed through its `paths` escape hatch, same as
 *  BcnSetupWizardCard uses for the wizard's own mark. */
const COMPASS =
  '<path d="m16.24 7.76-1.804 5.411a2 2 0 0 1-1.265 1.265L7.76 16.24l1.804-5.411a2 2 0 0 1 1.265-1.265z"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>';
---

<!-- manifest:
  layout: AppShell > PageLayout > stack(lg)              # SPINE
  sections:
    - review progress -> bcn-registry-progress           # decided share + the 613-vs-402 reconciliation
    - the registry    -> bcn-registry-tree               # nested: 17 areas > 69 headings > 402 duties; mode="review"
  title-badge: esa-badge                                 # which setup step this is
-->

<AppShell
  title="Project setup — Obligations — Beacon"
  tenantName="DWR"
  projectName="Delta Conveyance"
  activeNavId="setup-wizard"
>
  <PageLayout
    title="Obligations"
    icon="compass"
    iconPaths={COMPASS}
    breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Setup Wizard', href: '#project-setup' }, { label: 'Obligations' }]}
  >
    <Fragment slot="title-badge">
      <EsaBadge value="Step 5 of 5" variant="secondary" size="sm" />
    </Fragment>

    <div class="stack" data-gap="lg">
      <BcnRegistryProgress />
      <BcnRegistryTree mode="review" />
    </div>
  </PageLayout>
</AppShell>

<script>
  import { initRegistryTree } from '../../components/bcn/registry-tree';
  initRegistryTree();
</script>
